  Return 7.1% a year, volatility 8.5%, Sharpe ratio 0.57, worst fall −26.6%.
  Held from the start and rebalanced quarterly, without trading costs. In
  sample, so it flatters the weights: a backtest tests them on returns they had
  not seen.

How the weights were found
──────────────────────────
  Method      Maximum Sharpe: the largest expected return in excess of the
              risk-free rate per unit of volatility, solved as a quadratic
              programme after the Charnes–Cooper transformation.
  Data        US Equity, Intl Equity, Bonds, Gold and Real Estate; T-Bills as
              the risk-free asset. Monthly returns, Nov 1996 to Oct 2026.
  Estimates   Historical mean expected returns and the Ledoit–Wolf covariance,
              annualised by 12.
  Limits      At most 60% in any asset.
  Resampling  The average of the optimal weights for 50 histories of 360 monthly
              returns simulated from the estimates (Michaud, 1998); 50 of them
              solved.
  Solver      Interior point, optimal after 11 iterations.

